K325T a1200-fastcpu: the fast 68020 (cpucfg bit 3): the 68EC020's throttle off, its cache over fast RAM
With cpucfg[2] (the A1200's 68EC020: AGA + 68020) the cpu_cycles model holds the TG68K by the
68EC020's manual cycle counts.  Until now the throttle could only be dropped together with ec020
(cpucfg[2] = 0), which also drops the 24-bit address bus and the instruction cache: chip-RAM code
then runs 4x slower than the stock A1200 (every opcode word a chip-bus cycle).  Bit 3 (MiSTer's
68030 bits b4:b3 are not used in this port) is the fast setting:
- stock_speed = 0: cpu_cycles mode 0 (run = 0, hold = 0); ec020, the instruction cache and the
  32-bit chip cycles stay;
- the instruction cache also serves code in fast RAM, as a real 68020's does (the stock model
  caches chip RAM only and lets other fetches evict entries, nf_v): a fast-RAM fetch waits one clock
  for the lookup (ramsel held off), a hit is answered through the chip FSM's ic_take path (fic_take),
  a miss goes to the fast RAM, and the high + low word fetches of one longword fill its entry (ff_*).
  fr_busy keeps ramsel up while a fast-RAM access is open (the parent answers every started access).
Kickstart code ($F80000-$FFFFFF) is cached too in the fast setting (ifrom; fills by the chip FSM's
32-bit ROM reads, patch 0132).  Stage F2: cpucfg bit 4 (set with bit 3 by the parent) = the look-ahead
lookup: when a fetch of a low word completes, the cache is read for the next longword, so sequential
code is fetched at one clock per word.
With cpucfg[4:3] = 0 the module is unchanged (fifch = 0, lk_a = the current address: ramsel, nf_v,
the lookup and the FSM as before).
